#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0$.\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x\\right) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\ln 3 \\right) \\\\ &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0 \\\\ &= 3 e^x. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\\ &= 4 e^x+ 3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\\ &= 4 e^x+ 12x^{3} - 2x. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14e68965-6c39-5947-ac05-eee99e685063" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln 2 \\\\ &= 3 \\ln 2 \\cdot 2^{x}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
